    "markdown": "# Leadership and the Project Manager\n\n\n\n::: {.callout-note}\nThis chapter is in preparation. It will be built from **Lecture 4 -- Leadership\nand Project Management** of MGMT-3105, following the same structure as\n[Chapter 1](01-Sustainability.qmd) and\n[Chapter 9](09-Cost-Estimation-and-Budgeting.qmd).\n:::\n\n## Learning objectives (planned)\n\nBy the end of this chapter you will be able to:\n\n- Distinguish management from leadership in a project context.\n- Describe the sources of a project manager's power (legitimate, reward, coercive, expert, referent).\n- Apply situational leadership: matching style (directing, coaching, supporting, delegating) to team maturity.\n- Explain the stages of team development (forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning).\n- Identify the competencies and ethical responsibilities of an effective project manager.\n- Use motivation theory (Maslow, Herzberg, expectancy) to diagnose team performance issues.\n\n## Topic outline\n\n1. The project manager's role across the life cycle.\n2. Power, influence, and authority without ownership of resources.\n3. Leadership styles and situational leadership.\n4. Building and developing the project team.\n5. Motivation and engagement.\n6. Emotional intelligence and ethics.\n7. Running case: staffing and leading the RC-vehicle project team.\n",
